  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe previous forecast of heavy rain is still on track; and some\nlocations have already received 1 to 2\". The remnants of \nHurricane Polo will pass from south to north today, with the \nsteady rains ending during mid morning (Liberal), midday (Dodge\nCity) and late afternoon (Hays). Given the extremely anomalous \nprecipitable water values for this time of year, along with the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo and upper level trough, excessive \nrains are likely. Thus, a the flood watch remains in effect \nthrough Thursday. Given the heavy rains in Pawnee County\nTuesday, this county was added to the watch.\n\nAs drier air arrives at mid levels in the wake of the heavy rain,\nat least some insolation will allow for destabilization as \ntemperatures warm from the upper 60s at sunrise into the mid \n70s. Surface based t-storm development should happen provided \nthat this modest warming does occur. Given the tropical-like low\nlevel airmass and lack of a mid level capping inversion, it \nwon't take much heating for storm initiation. Expect initial \nstorms to the south and west of Dodge City by mid day, with the \nstorm chances shifting to the north and east during the \nafternoon and evening. Given the expected low cloud bases, with \ntemperatures in the 70s and dewpoints in the upper 60s to near \n70, along with a veering wind profile and adequate low level \nSRH, a few small tornadoes are likely. Backed winds to the \nnortheast of the surface low pressure could enhance low level \nSRH. Although violent tornadoes are not expected in this \npattern; even a small tornado from a miniature, low-topped\nsupercell can do significant damage, albeit in a very narrow \nswath.\n\nEven after the heavier rain is over, the upper level low will\nmove very slowly so that additional light to moderate rain is \nforecast for Thursday. Rain totals through Thursday could reach \n3 to 4\" or even locally higher, especially for locations that \nreceived heavy rains Tuesday. 75-90th percentiles of the various\nensembles support these localized, higher-end amounts.\n\nSurface high pressure will move southward out of Canada into the\nplains by Friday. With light winds and clear skies Friday night,\ntemperatures will fall into the 40s, with some readings near 40\nin west central Kansas. Of course, these readings are not that \nunusual for this time of year.",
